      "worked": "For $x\\ge-\\frac12$, both $x+1\\ge0$ and $2x+1\\ge0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=\\left|x+(x+1)\\right|\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=|2x+1|\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=2x+1\\end{align*}$$\nFor $-1\\le x<-\\frac12$, $x+1\\ge0$ but $2x+1<0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=\\left|x+(x+1)\\right|\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=|2x+1|\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=-(2x+1)\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=-2x-1\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x<-1$, $x+1<0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=\\left|x-(x+1)\\right|\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=|-1|\\\\\\left|x+\\lvert x+1\\rvert\\right|&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThese three cases establish the stated identity for every real $x$.\nThe graph has a horizontal left ray $y=1$ for $x<-1$, a segment $y=-2x-1$ from $(-1,1)$ to $\\left(-\\frac12,0\\right)$, and a right ray $y=2x+1$ through $(0,1)$.\nAt $x=-1$, the adjacent expressions both give $1$; at $x=-\\frac12$, they both give $0$. Both joins are continuous, with no holes. The dashed negative portion of $y=x+|x+1|$ is reflected in the $x$-axis to obtain the solid graph.",
